{align:justify|People in Shanghai have a saying ever on their lips: "retribution in this very life" — meaning what you do in the present is repaid in this present life, without waiting for the next. In Shanghai there was a Mr. Vuong who loved eating turtle meat, but his way of cooking was most peculiar: he filled a pot with water and sealed it with a lid — a hole bored in the lid's center, just big enough for a turtle's head. He put the live turtle in, then lit the fire. As the water slowly heated, the turtle, tormented beyond bearing by the boiling, poked its head through the hole, gasping for air — whereupon he poured his pre-made, still-boiling seasoned sauce into the turtle's mouth, forcing it to pull its head back in. His aim was to let the seasoning soak through the turtle's whole body, making the meat delicious. But never did he spare a thought for the turtle's suffering: inside, boiling water scalding its vitals; outside, roasting flames. Upon the living turtle he inflicted torment from within and without — utterly savage. Nor was that all: writhing and thrashing in agony in the hot water, unable to bear the boiling torture, with no door to flee by and no road to life, it could only poke its head through the lid's hole to cool off. Then cruel Mr. Vuong poured on more heated sauce — salt, oil, vinegar, chili, pepper — and the turtle, its insides burning, jerked its head down. The poking out and pulling in repeated again and again until at last the turtle died in uttermost torment. For greedy love of tasty food, Mr. Vuong could kill so ruthlessly.}
{align:justify|Then Mr. Vuong enjoyed his turtle dish. A dozen days later, one night as he slept in his upstairs room, the ground floor suddenly caught fire. Flames devoured the staircase and spread to his bedroom, reaching the bedclothes before he startled awake. In confusion and panic he meant to flee downstairs — but the wooden stairs had burned away. Below lay a sea of fire; no way down. He tried to squeeze through the window to save himself, but the iron bars' gaps were too narrow: strain as he might, again and again he could not pass — only his head poked through, wedged fast there, unable to advance or retreat.}
{align:justify|As he screamed, the fire engines came. Fierce flames flooded the ground; his head, trapped in the iron bars as if roasting hotter still, gaped wide, howling for rescue, while the fireman aimed an unceasing jet at the barred window — and scalding water shot into his mouth, painful exactly as when he had tormented the turtle long before. The head stuck beyond the bars kept gulping boiling water, and then perhaps he truly tasted the pitiful torment the turtle had known. And so Mr. Vuong died at last in the sea of flames.}
{align:justify|His way of killing and eating the turtle made him taste an identical torment — karmic retribution never lies. As we use an evil heart to kill creatures in life, that very evil heart harms us in turn. Mr. Vuong cast himself into the furnace of water and fire.}
{align:justify|The Dhammapada records:}
{align:justify|"When evil meets with fortune, its wickedness is not yet ripe; when the evil fruit ripens, one suffers cruel punishment oneself." Mr. Vuong's reaping of retribution truly chills the heart; the manner of his death was pain beyond bearing. Know that however we kill any animal, an equal retribution returns. May all who love killing reflect and take heed.}
